{parent=Paper 27}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

An <ultraproduct> assembles a family of nonempty <first-order structures> $\langle M_i:i\in I\rangle$ in one fixed language, using an <ultrafilter> $U$ to specify which coordinate statements hold almost everywhere. Its universe is
$$
\prod_{i\in I}M_i/U,\qquad
f\sim_Ug\ \Longleftrightarrow\ \{i:f(i)=g(i)\}\in U.
$$
Functions are interpreted coordinatewise, and an $r$-ary relation holds on $[f_1],\ldots,[f_r]$ when its coordinate truth set belongs to $U$. Changing representatives changes such a truth set only off a $U$-large set, so these definitions are well defined. A principal <ultrafilter> simply recovers its distinguished factor; a <nonprincipal ultrafilter> can produce new elements and structures.

The fundamental result is the <Łoś theorem>:
$$
\prod_iM_i/U\models\varphi([f_1],\ldots,[f_r])
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\{i:M_i\models\varphi(f_1(i),\ldots,f_r(i))\}\in U.
$$
Prove it by <structural induction> on <first-order formulas>. Atomic formulas are the definition. Intersections handle conjunction, while exactly one of a set and its complement belongs to an <ultrafilter>, handling negation. For the existential step, if the coordinate existential truth set is in $U$, use <choice> to select witnesses there and arbitrary values elsewhere; their product function is an ultraproduct witness. Conversely a product witness makes the coordinate existential truth set contain a $U$-large set. Other connectives and universal quantification follow.

In an <ultrapower>, all factors are one structure $M$. The diagonal map $a\mapsto[\text{constant }a]$ is an <elementary embedding> by the <Łoś theorem>. For a nonprincipal $U$ on $\omega$, the class of $n\mapsto n$ in an ultrapower of the standard <natural numbers> is larger than every constant natural number, since each tail belongs to $U$. This gives a <nonstandard model of Peano arithmetic>. Similarly, an ultraproduct of finite <fields> $\mathbb F_{p_i}$ with distinct primes tending to infinity has <characteristic zero>: for every fixed positive integer $m$, the equation $m1=0$ fails in all sufficiently late factors. An infinite structure can therefore arise from finite factors.

Ultraproducts prove the <compactness theorem>. If each finite subset $\Delta$ of a theory $T$ has a model $M_\Delta$, index these models by $[T]^{<\omega}$. The cones $\{\Delta:\varphi\in\Delta\}$ have the <finite intersection property>. Extend their filter to an <ultrafilter> using the <ultrafilter lemma>. The <Łoś theorem> makes the resulting ultraproduct satisfy every $\varphi\in T$. This construction also gives elementary extensions realizing prescribed finitely satisfiable types.

A useful stronger property is <countable saturation of an ultraproduct over omega>. Let a countable type $\{\varphi_n(x,\bar a_n):n<\omega\}$ be finitely satisfiable in an ultraproduct over a nonprincipal $U$ on $\omega$. Choose coordinate representatives of its parameters. Let $E_n$ be the coordinates where the first $n$ formulas have a simultaneous witness; then $E_n\in U$, the $E_n$ decrease, and we can replace $E_n$ by $E_n\cap[n,\infty)$. At coordinate $i$, choose a witness to the first $m(i)$ formulas, where $m(i)$ is the largest $n\le i$ with $i\in E_n$, taking an arbitrary value if there is none. For each fixed $n$, the set where $m(i)\ge n$ is $U$-large. Its class realizes the entire type. This proof uses countable incompleteness of $U$, not arbitrary completeness or automatic saturation at larger cardinals.

Finally, complete <ultrafilters> connect ultraproducts to large <cardinals>. A countably complete <ultrafilter> prevents an externally infinite descending membership chain in an ultrapower of a well-founded universe: intersect the countably many coordinate truth sets and obtain an impossible descending chain at one coordinate. A <kappa-complete> nonprincipal <ultrafilter> on $\kappa$ consequently supplies the <ultrapower embedding> associated with a <measurable cardinal>. Thus the same construction supports both elementary compactness arguments and large-cardinal embeddings, with the completeness hypothesis deciding which well-foundedness and closure properties survive.

